These scripts are used to manage a set of AWS instances for the workshop.

EC2 dashboard
-------------

Install the script dependencies with Python 3.10 or newer, then open the TUI:

    python -m pip install -r scripts/requirements.txt
    python scripts/dashboard.py

To select a group initially or change the 30-second refresh interval:

    python scripts/dashboard.py --group student --refresh-interval 60

The dashboard uses the same AWS credentials and us-east-1 region as the launcher.
To use a named AWS profile, set AWS_PROFILE when starting it:

    AWS_PROFILE=course python scripts/dashboard.py

Groups are discovered from ws_group tags. Select student, instructor, or another
group in the dropdown. The table shows hostname, state, EC2 system and instance
checks, private IP, instance ID, type, and public DNS. Stopped and pending
instances are included; terminated instances are excluded. Use the arrow keys
to highlight a row and scroll horizontally. The highlighted instance's addresses
and ID also appear below the table.

Keyboard shortcuts (buttons also support the mouse):

    g       Open the group selector
    r       Refresh now
    Space   Select or deselect the highlighted row (Enter or a click also works)
    a       Select all eligible instances in the current group
    c       Clear the selection
    t       Terminate the selected instances, after confirmation
    b       Reboot the selected running instances, after confirmation
    s       Start the selected stopped instances, after confirmation
    S       Stop the selected running instances, after confirmation (Shift+S)
    n       Launch a new group, or add instances to an existing group
    l       Show or hide the activity log
    q       Quit
    Escape  Cancel a dialog

Reboot requests use EC2 RebootInstances. EC2 can continue to show "running"
during a reboot. AWS attempts a hard reboot if the instance cannot shut down
cleanly within a few minutes. A reboot interrupts the student's session and
clears kernel memory; saved files remain on disk. EC2 checks report host and OS
health, so a notebook can hang while both checks report "ok". The dashboard does
not measure memory usage or test Jupyter responsiveness. See:

    https://docs.aws.amazon.com/boto3/latest/reference/services/ec2/client/reboot_instances.html
    https://docs.aws.amazon.com/boto3/latest/reference/services/ec2/client/describe_instance_status.html

The Select column marks instances for Start, Stop, Reboot, and Terminate.
Selections survive refreshes and clear when switching groups. Instances that
disappear or begin shutting down are removed from the selection. All four
actions require at least one checked row. Use Select all (a) followed by an
action to operate on the current group. Start requires every selected instance
to be stopped; Stop and Reboot require every selected instance to be running.
Deselect instances in other states before choosing those actions.

Start, Stop, Reboot, and Terminate appear together beside the selection controls.
Each action opens a confirmation showing the exact selected hostnames, instance
IDs, group, and count. Termination is permanent;
disks configured for deletion on termination, including notebook files on those
disks, are deleted. Back up student work first. See:

    https://docs.aws.amazon.com/boto3/latest/reference/services/ec2/client/terminate_instances.html

Stop uses EC2's normal shutdown request. Stopping clears kernel memory and
interrupts notebook sessions; files on EBS disks remain available after starting
again. Wait for the stopped state before using Start. Starting resumes EC2
compute charges, and Jupyter may need a little longer to finish starting. See:

    https://docs.aws.amazon.com/boto3/latest/reference/services/ec2/client/stop_instances.html
    https://docs.aws.amazon.com/boto3/latest/reference/services/ec2/client/start_instances.html

The dashboard rechecks the reviewed IDs, group tags, and states before sending
any action requests. All four actions apply to the instances listed in
the confirmation; instances launched later are excluded. Successful requests
clear the selection; failed requests keep it for review. The dashboard keeps
the empty group selected after termination.
AWS termination protection remains in effect. If a request fails, some
instances may already be terminating; refresh the table before retrying.
Termination leaves the existing NGINX proxy configuration in place.

Launch group asks for a group name and count, shows the configured AMI and
instance type, and runs launch_instances.py with those settings. Proxy updates
are enabled by default; select "Skip NGINX proxy update" to omit that step.
Launcher output and errors open the Activity panel. Press l to hide it and give
the instance table more room. If a launch partially fails,
instances that were already created remain in AWS. Check the table and output
before retrying. If only the proxy update failed, run update_proxy.py GROUP.
Run one launcher per group at a time, including launchers in other terminals.
Wait for an active operation to finish before quitting the dashboard.

EC2 queries run in a background worker without overlapping refreshes. Each
refresh reads the tagged instances and batches health queries for running
instances (at most 100 IDs per request), following all response pages. The UI
sleeps between updates. The minimum refresh interval is 5 seconds. A failed
refresh keeps the last table visible, marks it STALE, and disables instance
actions until a refresh succeeds. If only health queries fail, addresses and
states still update and unavailable checks appear as "unknown".

AWS access needs ec2:DescribeInstances and ec2:DescribeInstanceStatus for the
table, ec2:RebootInstances for reboot, ec2:StartInstances for start,
ec2:StopInstances for stop, and ec2:TerminateInstances for termination. Launch
uses the existing launcher's EC2 permissions and proxy SSH configuration.

Run the local tests with the dependencies installed (AWS calls are stubbed):

    python -m unittest discover -s scripts/tests -v

Script workflow
---------------

The command-line workflow is:

 1: (no scripts for this) start the proxy server(s) and the server for the URL
    app.

 2: Pick a name for a group of instances. The name itself does not matter; it's
    just a bookkeeping device. Ex: "hydrogen"

 3: Run launch_instances.py to start the servers that host the notebooks:

        python scripts/launch_instances.py hydrogen 10

    Each instance gets ws_group=hydrogen and a unique ws_hostname such as
    hydrogen0 during launch. Existing names in the group are preserved and
    skipped. Run only one launcher per group at a time to avoid duplicate names.

    The launcher uses the private IPs returned at launch to update and reload
    the NGINX proxy immediately, including while instances are still pending.
    If an address is missing, it checks EC2 for that instance for up to about a
    minute. It never waits for the running state. Delayed group-tag visibility
    cannot omit the new instances from the proxy configuration. Other pending
    and running instances in the group are included too.

    Jupyter starts automatically on the new NEA AMI and may need a little longer
    to finish starting after the proxy is updated.

Use --skip-proxy to launch and tag instances without changing NGINX:

    python scripts/launch_instances.py hydrogen 10 --skip-proxy

update_proxy.py remains available to retry a failed proxy update or refresh the
group after other changes. A failed proxy update leaves the launched instances
in place; rerun only the proxy command to avoid launching additional instances:

    python scripts/update_proxy.py hydrogen

The proxy configuration is streamed over SSH, so no local .conf file is created.
NGINX configuration is checked before reload, and the previous configuration file
is restored if installation, checking, or reloading fails. SSH errors are reported
as failures. Both scripts use the proxy address and SSH key in update_proxy.py.

The new NEA AMI starts Jupyter automatically through nea-jupyter.service.
For older AMIs, wait for SSH to be ready and run start_nb_servers.py GROUP
before using the notebooks. That script starts Jupyter through run_jupyter.sh;
it does not currently update the course checkout.

add_hostnames.py GROUP remains available for assigning names to older instances
that are missing ws_hostname tags. It is unnecessary after the updated launcher.

To launch one vanilla Ubuntu 26.04 amd64 builder for a new course AMI:

    python scripts/launch_ubuntu_instance.py nea-build

This resolves Canonical's current stable AMI in us-east-1, uses the existing
key pair and security group, and allocates a 64-GiB gp3 root volume. Use
--root-volume-gb 32 for a 32-GiB root volume or --dry-run to check launch
permissions without creating an instance. See --help for other options.
Copy for_notebook_server/nea to the builder and run its setup_ami.sh separately.
